{"kind":"report","text":"DEPLOYMENT ROUND 8 CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head d1460aed0059ff072646c0117d071fba44fac4cf, against request git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3135eec7b9490f8be6f3c744eb403b9201e2ae14 and review promise git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:038eba11ab5cd0a640c3d648c426c9a70d60a9e6.\n\nFour of the five round-7 blockers are mechanically closed. Independent Git plumbing at /tmp/gitseq-deployment-r8.9mPV0U confirms a ledger commit whose parents are E and both candidates retains all three after reflog expiry and gc --prune=now. It also confirms the race claim precisely: with remote refs/seq advanced, the lease without a sequence refspec exited 0, created the claim and deleted inbox; the same atomic push with explicit P:refs/seq/g exited 1 and left inbox present, claim absent and ledger absent; after restoring remote seq=P, that explicit-no-op command exited 0. The table now names no-claim equal/accounted, equal/unaccounted and divergent states. Case 7 now has the necessary same-credential positive control. Cases 8 and 10, and the other spike cases, remain capable of failing for their named subjects under the prior audit.\n\nOne mechanism-level terminology blocker remains in two normative places. The repaired protocol explicitly rejects refs/notes/gitseq-batches because a note does not retain E/candidates, but the quiescent precondition at line 452 still requires “no unaccounted batch note,” and case 6 at lines 667-670 still says destroyed claims are caught by “the retained batch note.” Both must say ledger entry/ledger branch; leaving the obsolete object in the state-machine precondition and executable spike permits an implementation to satisfy those clauses with the mechanism this head has just proved unsound.\n\nFor an executable case 7, also name the byte-identity baseline as the snapshot taken after the allowed inbox positive control (or remove that control ref before comparing). As written,